data.content:
## Migration Analysis

Your code has **three** thread-termination patterns that break on .NET 8:

| API | Location | Pattern | .NET 8 Behavior |
|-----|----------|---------|-----------------|
| `Thread.Interrupt()` | `Stop()` | Blocking call interruption | Obsolete (`SYSLIB0046` in .NET 9), unreliable |
| `Thread.Abort()` | `Stop()` | Forcible termination fallback | **Throws `PlatformNotSupportedException`** |
| `ManualResetEvent.WaitOne()` | `RunLoop()` | Blocking call that needs waking | Not cancellation-aware |

There's also a **race condition bug** in the original: multiple items can be enqueued before `WaitOne` returns, but only one is dequeued per signal — items get stuck.

---

## Migrated Code (.NET 8)

```csharp
public class EventProcessor : IDisposable
{
    private readonly CancellationTokenSource _cts = new();
    private readonly BlockingCollection<string> _queue = new();
    private Thread? _thread;

    public void Start()
    {
        _thread = new Thread(RunLoop);
        _thread.Start();
    }

    private void RunLoop()
    {
        try
        {
            // GetConsumingEnumerable blocks until an item arrives
            // and throws OperationCanceledException when the token fires.
            foreach (var item in _queue.GetConsumingEnumerable(_cts.Token))
            {
                Handle(item);
            }
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException)
        {
            // Normal shutdown — nothing to do.
        }
    }

    public void Enqueue(string item)
    {
        _queue.Add(item);
    }

    public void Stop()
    {
        // Signal the loop to stop (cooperative cancellation).
        _cts.Cancel();

        // Wait up to 5 seconds for the thread to finish.
        _thread?.Join(5000);
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        Stop();
        _cts.Dispose();
        _queue.Dispose();
    }

    private void Handle(string item) { /* ... */ }
}
```

### What changed and why

| Before (.NET Framework) | After (.NET 8) | Why |
|---|---|---|
| `ManualResetEvent` + `lock` + `Queue<T>` | `BlockingCollection<string>` | Thread-safe producer-consumer collection; eliminates the manual signaling, locking, and the race condition bug |
| `Thread.Interrupt()` to wake `WaitOne` | `CancellationToken` passed to `GetConsumingEnumerable` | Cooperative cancellation — the blocked call wakes cleanly when the token fires |
| `Thread.Abort()` as fallback kill | Removed entirely | **Throws `PlatformNotSupportedException`** on .NET 8. Cooperative cancellation makes it unnecessary |
| No cleanup | `IDisposable` | `CancellationTokenSource` and `BlockingCollection` are `IDisposable` and must be disposed to avoid timer/handle leaks |

### Key pitfalls to watch for

1. **`Thread.Abort` is not a fallback** — there is no way to forcibly kill a managed thread in .NET 8. If `Handle()` can hang, add `_cts.Token` checks inside it or use async I/O with token support.
2. **Dispose `CancellationTokenSource`** — leaking it causes timer and callback leaks.
3. **Don't swallow `OperationCanceledException`** deeper in the call chain — only catch it at the top-level loop.
4. **If `Handle()` does long work**, pass `_cts.Token` through so cancellation is responsive, not just checked between items.
